WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:06.340
SIERRA: We think we live in a world with one
set of physical rules.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.660
If I’m holding an apple and I let it go,
it will fall to the floor.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:16.250
SIERRA: But at the smallest scale - inside
of molecules and atoms - there’s another

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:20.670
world, where normal rules of physics cease
to exist.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:25.789
A world where gravity operates differently,
where a single particle can be in more than

00:00:25.789 --> 00:00:28.380
one place at a time.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.170
This is the quantum world.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:32.509
And it is happening all around us.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:34.940
Avengers Endgame: The Quantum realm is like
its own microscopic universe.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.270
To get in there you have to be incredibly
small.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.710
Neil deGrasse Tyson: Particles pop in and
out of existence.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:43.710
You try to measure it and it is not there
anymore, but you saw it there a moment ago.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:49.380
Richard Feynman: The behavior of things on
a small scale is so fantastic, it is so wonderfully

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:55.460
different, so marvelously different than anything
that behaves on a large scale.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.610
SIERRA: It’s kind of like magic, but real.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:04.290
SIERRA: And right now, research teams around
the world are in the process of harnessing

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:08.190
this magic to build quantum computers.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:14.220
And that means that you and I, dear listener,
are living during the start of a technological

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.550
revolution, just like the space age, or the
nuclear age in the past.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:24.659
Some experts believe that a useful quantum
computer could be complete in just a decade.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:29.659
And when they are ready, certain calculations
that would take tens of thousands of years

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:33.440
on a regular supercomputer could be solvable
in minutes.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.079
SIERRA: Quantum computers will change the
world.

00:01:37.079 --> 00:01:41.290
And we are only just starting to ask the right
questions about what that will mean.

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:45.290
SIERRA: I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is
Why It Matters.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:50.890
Today, de-mystifying quantum computers.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:56.970
GHOSE: Quantum computers are a completely
different technology, they're a whole new

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.409
type of approach to doing computing.

00:02:00.409 --> 00:02:05.159
And for that, we need to rethink what we even
mean by computing itself.

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:07.710
SIERRA: This is Shohini Ghose.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:12.960
She is a quantum physicist and a professor
at Wilfrid Laurier University in Waterloo,

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:13.960
Canada.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:19.270
GIUSTINA: Imagine you live in a chess board
or a checker board and your rule set is that

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:22.960
you can only move on horizontal or vertical
axes.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.630
SIERRA: And this is Marissa Guistina.

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:30.390
She’s a research scientist and quantum electronics
engineer at Google.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:34.000
GIUSTINA: You can go forward and backwards,
left and right and that's all you know and

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.300
suppose you suddenly had access to another
rule set that allows you to move on the diagonals,

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:43.500
well, you're going to be able to get from
one to the opposite corner much faster if

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.470
you live in the world that allows you to use
diagonals than if you live in the world that

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:50.970
only allows you to move up and down, left
and right.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.870
In our everyday lives, we are accustomed to
the rules of the way the world works.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.720
You drop something you expect it's going to
fall.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:58.720
Gravity's going to pull it down.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:02:59.830
You don't think about that.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:04.420
You take it for granted, but you could imagine
that if you had access to another universe,

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.890
if you will, with a different set of rules,
you might be able to build different tools,

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.560
and quantum computing is just that.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.220
It's the idea that maybe if we climb into
the set of rules given to us by quantum mechanics,

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:20.370
we could build a tool that behaves totally
differently from the tools in our everyday

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.220
world that maybe is better at solving some
problems.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.220
SIERRA: It's like a multiverse.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:25.220
GIUSTINA: Oh, let's not get an to multiverses.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:26.220
Yeah.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.620
SIERRA: You're like, "Please don't say that."

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:33.560
GIUSTINA: Then we get into conflicts between
different interpretations of quantum mechanics

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:34.560
and it gets very philosophical.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:35.560
SIERRA: Oh, my God.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:36.560
Okay.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.760
I don't want to start a rumble in the quantum
mechanics community, please.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.290
I'll back it up.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:46.060
SIERRA: Listen, if there’s one thing that
I can tell you from making this episode, is

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.650
that if it’s quantum, it’s confusing.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.170
But one of the best ways to understand what
quantum computers are for is to compare them

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:57.400
to the standard computers that we’re all
used to.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:02.159
GHOSE: So standard computing is actually pretty
easy because everything boils down to combining

00:04:02.159 --> 00:04:03.560
zeros and ones.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:09.550
SIERRA: Regular computers think in a binary
system using something called bits.

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:14.530
Bits are sort of like switches, and at any
given time, they can either be a zero or a

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:15.530
one.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:16.530
That’s it.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:20.570
Everything your computer does happens because
it is working its way through millions of

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:22.019
these zeros and ones.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:27.300
GHOSE: All information, whether you're writing
emails, or doing some fancy calculation to

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.470
launch a rocket ship to Mars, all calculations
and information and data are just zeros and

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:33.510
ones.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:38.530
But with quantum computing, the idea is that
we move away from restricting ourselves just

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:43.830
to zero and one, because if you think about
it, that's a pretty small space to start computing

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:44.830
in.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:50.730
Instead if we can expand and think about combinations
of zeros and ones where you have some probability

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:55.190
of being zero and some probability of one,
then that opens up a much broader range.

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:59.789
And it's a little more fuzzy of course, because
we're not talking about precision zeros and

00:04:59.789 --> 00:05:00.789
ones.

00:05:00.789 --> 00:05:04.050
But on the other hand, you can think of it
as a much larger landscape to play in.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:09.430
SIERRA: Quantum computers aren’t constrained
by the binary system of bits.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.980
Instead, they use something called qubits.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:19.110
Rather than being a zero or a one, a qubit
can be in both states simultaneously.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:22.889
If you’re scratching your head right now,
you are in good company.

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:29.450
But one way to say it is that quantum computers
use the strange behaviors of sub-atomic particles

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:31.180
to solve problems.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:40.010
SIERRA: So what type of sectors, or I guess
people, will be using quantum computers?

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:46.800
GHOSE: It's hard to predict where technology
can be applied, but we do know very specific

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.950
kinds of algorithms that a quantum computer
would provide advantages for.

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:56.751
So let's say if you want to simulate the behavior
of a molecule for drug development, that's

00:05:56.751 --> 00:06:00.100
a quantum simulation, obviously you'd want
to use a quantum computer.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:04.310
GIUSTINA: You could almost think of a quantum
computer and as being a little bit like a

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:06.310
programmable molecule.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:12.130
If a chemist wants to model a molecule really
fully and they want to use a classical computer,

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:16.750
there's a pretty low limit as to how large
that molecule can get before we just can't

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.740
even run the simulation anymore.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:23.340
The number of parameters that you have to
keep track of gets really big, but that same

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:28.569
kind of complexity that the molecule has that's
difficult to model with a classical computer

00:06:28.569 --> 00:06:31.110
is actually built into the quantum computer.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:34.350
It's because of the quantum mechanics that
it's difficult to model classically.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:37.759
So you could almost say that the dynamics
of the molecule are the native language of

00:06:37.759 --> 00:06:39.229
the quantum computer.

00:06:39.229 --> 00:06:45.300
SIERRA: Right now, if you want to create a
new treatment for, say, Alzheimers, or Covid,

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:50.349
you have to conduct real-life experiments
in a lab for months or years.

00:06:50.349 --> 00:06:55.419
But with quantum computers, you could potentially
simulate many of those experiments in minutes

00:06:55.419 --> 00:07:01.390
or hours, by creating exact models of the
molecules that make up things like organs,

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:04.350
blood cells, or brain tissue.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:09.450
Experts are very conservative about projections,
but these types of simulations could lead

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:11.510
to faster breakthroughs.

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:16.840
GHOSE: But that's only one application of
quantum simulation.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:21.030
You can certainly use simulations to develop
let's say better solar cells, which are also

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:27.700
based on quantum properties of things like
how you can develop materials that can absorb

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:31.879
energy more efficiently and can store energy,
building better batteries, all of this.

00:07:31.879 --> 00:07:35.629
And that of course relates to things like
even transportation, because then you can

00:07:35.629 --> 00:07:38.230
talk about electric vehicles and things like
this.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:45.009
WEBER: One could imagine modeling the chemical
reactions required for nuclear fusion for

00:07:45.009 --> 00:07:49.350
fusion energy, which to Marissa's point, would
be transformational for climate change.

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:54.770
SIERRA: We also spoke to Kate Weber, she works
with Marissa, but on the policy end.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:57.409
WEBER: Do you want to start here Marissa?

00:07:57.409 --> 00:07:59.849
I’m sure Marissa has thoughts that she…

00:07:59.849 --> 00:08:03.580
SIERRA: She is a Policy Lead for Quantum Computing
at Google.

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:06.940
WEBER: I did a PhD in molecular biology.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:13.370
If I had been able to model protein interactions,
a lot of the work that I needed to do and

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:17.430
spent countless hours in the lab doing would
become kind of unnecessary.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:22.470
I could just have predicted the results using
a quantum computer, so.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:28.900
GHOSE: There's a whole general area around
optimization, finding the best solution or

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:30.930
the maximum or the minimum of functions.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:35.290
Now, that sounds very mathematical, but in
the real world, what that means is that the

00:08:35.290 --> 00:08:40.390
best solution could be, let's say, the best
transportation route for food supplies around

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:41.390
the world.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:46.700
By the time they come online, they will be
able to impact so many different areas of

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:47.700
our society.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.570
Whether we're talking, as I said, security
or healthcare, or even climate change questions,

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:57.830
anything that involves large amounts of information
processing for certain types of algorithms

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:04.470
would be open to the use of quantum computers,
but also perhaps in ways that are unexpected.

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:10.700
SIERRA: Functional quantum computers could
fundamentally alter any field or industry.

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:16.190
Finance, city planning, weather forecasting
- you name it!

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:24.470
SIERRA: Will there ever be a day, you know,
in the future when everyone is walking around

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:27.089
with a quantum computer in their back pocket?

00:09:27.089 --> 00:09:30.780
GIUSTINA: You can't think about a quantum
computer as something that will ever replace

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:31.780
a classical computer.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:36.209
It's a piece of additional hardware that helps
your computer do something that it could otherwise

00:09:36.209 --> 00:09:40.690
never do, but that aid alone is not the whole
picture.

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:44.910
So quantum computers, for example, are not
very good at counting or arithmetic.

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:49.511
Those are tasks that are really best left
for the standard classical computer, but there

00:09:49.511 --> 00:09:54.250
are other problems like chemical modeling,
for example, that are simply impossible for

00:09:54.250 --> 00:10:00.380
a classical computer by itself, and when partnered
up with a capable quantum computer, then the

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.839
real magic happens.

00:10:02.839 --> 00:10:10.269
A way to think about this could be that imagine
we're trying to develop new means of transportation

00:10:10.269 --> 00:10:12.000
and we want to get to the moon.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:16.160
Okay, we know we're going to need to develop
some pretty crazy technology to get to the

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:20.600
moon, time goes on, we invent the rocket,
we get to the moon, hooray, but that doesn't

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:22.600
mean that we now take rockets everywhere.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:27.430
If you want to go to the grocery store, your
bicycle is still a better bet than the rocket.

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:31.410
And similar with a quantum computer, we'll
need that rocket in order to solve certain

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:36.680
kinds of problems, but it's not going to replace
all of the other very effective computation

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:39.930
tools that we rely on.

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:46.740
SIERRA: Can you give me a picture of what
a quantum computer looks like?

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:50.130
GHOSE: I can give you a picture of a type
of quantum computer.

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:53.910
What's interesting is that the technology
is so new that we don't even know what it

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:55.610
will actually be based on.

00:10:55.610 --> 00:11:01.529
Will it be based on the solid state technologies
that we already have, for example, semiconductor

00:11:01.529 --> 00:11:07.630
kinds of approaches and materials, or will
we be using laser beams and photonics, or

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:13.639
in fact, some of the earliest approaches to
quantum computers was in liquid form because

00:11:13.639 --> 00:11:14.639
these were-

00:11:14.639 --> 00:11:15.639
SIERRA: Whoa.

00:11:15.639 --> 00:11:18.930
GHOSE: Yeah, these are molecules that were
in solution that were being controlled.

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:22.870
So that's how big the range is of what you
can visualize, maybe at which it'll be in

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:24.530
the form of a test tube.

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:25.530
SIERRA: Wow.

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:26.530
GHOSE: Who knows.

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:27.530
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:29.589
SIERRA: So these computers are already up
and running?

00:11:29.589 --> 00:11:34.860
GHOSE: Well, quantum computers, they do exist
in the sense that IBM for example, has a working

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:40.529
quantum computer, Google has one, and there
are also new startups that are building their

00:11:40.529 --> 00:11:42.060
own hardware.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:48.620
But this is sort of like saying we have computers
back in the days in the '60s, if you look

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:54.750
up history books or Google the earliest computers,
you'll get these photos of rooms full of these

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:56.630
dials and switches and-

00:11:56.630 --> 00:11:59.540
SIERRA: Totally like a whole room, very loud.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:00.540
Yeah.

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:03.400
Remington Rand Univac: In the heart of the
Univac, the electronic central computer, capable

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:08.470
of making over 2000 mathematical calculations
per second.

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:17.290
GHOSE: You know, when we see those photos,
we think of that as kind of primitive computers.

00:12:17.290 --> 00:12:20.730
And now we have super high tech, cool gadgets.

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:27.000
So I'd say that quantum computers that do
exist today are kind of like those old '60s-style,

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:28.000
regular computers.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:34.480
GIUSTINA: Some of the initial interesting
quantum computing started in the 80s and '90s

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:39.079
and really just kind of daydreaming about
what would it look like, but we also found

00:12:39.079 --> 00:12:43.510
that actually implementing that hardware is
extremely difficult, because of course we're

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:47.250
trying to build something that doesn't behave
according to our everyday rules.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:54.120
So actually very early on, it was found that
quantum information is fundamentally error

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:58.540
prone, you've got your quantum computer in
some state and then it just goes poof and

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:03.510
isn't there anymore, and in order to get around
that we need a technology known as quantum

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:04.850
error correction.

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:10.070
Any useful quantum computer is going to be
robust against these kinds of errors.

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:15.230
So now we're talking about needing millions
of physical qubits, and right now we've demonstrated

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:20.160
something on the order of tens to a hundred
of physical qubits.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:25.610
So we’re not only a ways away in terms of
the number of physical qubits that we need

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:28.100
to make, but also the performance that we
need to see.

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:32.629
So that's a ways out, but I will say that
not too long ago, we were saying, "Oh, it

00:13:32.629 --> 00:13:34.270
was 20-year out technology."

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.420
It was perpetually 20-year out technology.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:40.860
Now it's around a decade, so we're definitely
making progress, but there's also a lot of

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:42.650
progress still to come.

00:13:42.650 --> 00:13:47.329
SIERRA: With any new technology, of course,
there is the potential for misuse and risk.

00:13:47.329 --> 00:13:51.790
2001: A Space Odyssey: I know that you and
frank were planning to disconnect me and I

00:13:51.790 --> 00:13:54.949
am afraid that is something I cannot allow
to happen.

00:13:54.949 --> 00:13:57.620
SIERRA: Yeah no not that kind of risk.

00:13:57.620 --> 00:13:58.620
More like this:

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:04.680
TED, Vikram Sharma: And when quantum computers
arrive, in the next 10 - 15 years they will

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:11.399
even more rapidly crack the complex mathematics
that underlies many of our encryption systems

00:14:11.399 --> 00:14:12.399
today.

00:14:12.399 --> 00:14:19.670
Indeed the quantum computer is likely to turn
our present security castle into a mere house

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:20.670
of cards.

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:27.720
WEBER: The one very concrete risk that there's
been a lot of focus on is the predicted ability

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:34.500
of quantum computers to break RSA encryption
and there's a lot of work happening now to

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:35.930
get ahead of that.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:41.750
The U.S. National Institute of Standards in
Technology is running a standards process

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:44.180
to develop standards for post quantum cryptography.

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:49.509
Now these are cryptographic algorithms that
use classical computing, but are expected

00:14:49.509 --> 00:14:52.880
to be resistant to quantum computers.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:57.259
That is one specific risk that important to
think about and address now, because there

00:14:57.259 --> 00:15:04.230
is a possibility that adversaries could download
large amounts of encrypted information, store

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:10.430
it for 15 years and then when we have a fault
tolerant quantum computer that can break Shor's

00:15:10.430 --> 00:15:15.839
algorithm, which is what would break RSA encryption,
they could then decrypt that information.

00:15:15.839 --> 00:15:20.860
And if it's still important that it be kept
secret in a couple decades, it's sort of important

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:23.220
to think about protecting it now.

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:28.500
GHOSE: So a decade or two sounds like a long
time, but if you think about one thing, for

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:32.209
example, that we are all learning, especially
now during the pandemic, to be very careful

00:15:32.209 --> 00:15:36.880
about is healthcare, as well as healthcare
records and our information.

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:40.980
And if you think about the average person's
healthcare records, they don't expire in a

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:42.339
month or two months.

00:15:42.339 --> 00:15:43.769
We're talking about a lifetime.

00:15:43.769 --> 00:15:50.509
So 10 or 20 years may seem like it’s a long
time, but imagine if let's say 10 or 20 years

00:15:50.509 --> 00:15:55.509
from now somebody turns on a quantum computer
and can hack every single one of your passwords

00:15:55.509 --> 00:16:00.040
and take a look at all of your private data,
for everybody around the world, that would

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:01.040
be kind of bad.

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:05.410
And so if that is even a possibility now,
we should be thinking about it now and caring

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:11.279
about it now, meaning how do we protect against
that kind of situation, who gets to control

00:16:11.279 --> 00:16:17.490
that kind of technology, what else can you
use these kinds of quantum computers for?

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:22.079
SIERRA: So I'd love to pivot back a bit to
policy.

00:16:22.079 --> 00:16:28.420
So what are the biggest ethical or policy
concerns when it comes to quantum computing?

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:35.259
WEBER: So without knowing exactly what the
really powerful applications for quantum computing

00:16:35.259 --> 00:16:40.050
are going to be, it's really difficult to
identify what the biggest ethical concerns

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:41.190
will be.

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:45.350
But we can say that they'll come in a couple
of buckets.

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:49.990
And, I think when people think about this
now and say ethics, they actually are talking

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:51.139
about two things.

00:16:51.139 --> 00:16:55.420
One is, what are the applications for quantum
computing that raise concerns?

00:16:55.420 --> 00:17:00.400
And, then the other is related to this access
question, who has access to this technology

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:07.169
and will it further existing inequalities
in access to this to technology?

00:17:07.169 --> 00:17:11.770
So on the first, the lack of knowledge about
what the applications will be, means that

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:13.860
it's challenging to address now.

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:18.420
One of the reasons that I think we see so
much policymaker focus is there is a belief

00:17:18.420 --> 00:17:23.731
that there will be some defense-related applications
for the technology and, of course, that's

00:17:23.731 --> 00:17:28.980
where our view is that these frameworks for
thinking about emerging technology deployment

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:32.720
will become important as these applications
begin to be discovered.

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:38.130
SIERRA: We spoke to some experts about this
and there are national security concerns.

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:43.840
Again, the biggest issue is the predicted
ability of quantum computers to break existing

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:44.840
encryption.

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:49.030
SIERRA: It’s not just our personal data
at stake, quantum computers could also be

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:52.179
powerful tools for cyberwarfare.

00:17:52.179 --> 00:17:57.539
And there are other concerns too - they could
be used to develop counter-stealth technology,

00:17:57.539 --> 00:18:03.470
anti-submarine technology, military AI, and
could even be used to model more advanced

00:18:03.470 --> 00:18:04.610
nuclear weapons.

00:18:04.610 --> 00:18:09.970
SIERRA: But, just like in the civilian world,
the exact military and security implications

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:12.070
are not yet understood.

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:16.870
SIERRA: At the other end of the spectrum,
there are different policy concerns.

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:20.960
For example, who might get left out of the
quantum moment?

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:28.250
WEBER: On the access piece, it's also important
that we think about reaching communities that

00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:35.620
are less likely to have the resources to access
quantum computers or even through education

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:37.780
outreach efforts.

00:18:37.780 --> 00:18:43.280
Government funding is super important here,
both at the undergraduate, graduate education

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:48.110
level, but also the government has something
called the "Q12 Partnership," which is focused

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:56.419
on exposing even kids, school-age kids to
at least quantum computing and quantum technologies

00:18:56.419 --> 00:19:03.250
as a concept, the thinking being that the
earlier you broaden the pipeline, the more

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:07.850
likely you are to get people from underrepresented
groups interested.

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:10.140
GIUSTINA: Yeah, I was waiting from when I
was three.

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:11.240
SIERRA: So that's right.

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:12.240
Yeah.

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:13.240
That's funny.

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:18.220
WEBER: I don't think we've discussed the preschool
audience yet, Marissa, but I could try it

00:19:18.220 --> 00:19:20.440
out on my two-and-a-half and five-year-old.

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:21.440
SIERRA: There you go.

00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:25.880
WEBER: We have this Quantum Computing for
Babies book, actually, so..

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:28.280
GIUSTINA: I had seen that.

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:33.520
SIERRA: So obviously, you both work at Google,
but are there other companies that are working

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:34.520
on this?

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:39.520
I mean is this also something that the U.S.
Government is working on as well, whether

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:42.600
in partnership with private companies or within
the government?

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:48.409
WEBER: There are several other large tech
companies that are developing quantum computers

00:19:48.409 --> 00:19:49.409
too.

00:19:49.409 --> 00:19:52.200
There are Amazon, Microsoft, IBM.

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:57.799
Also, a lot of U.S. startups, several of whom
have recently gone public or will go public

00:19:57.799 --> 00:19:58.799
soon.

00:19:58.799 --> 00:20:04.289
So in terms of US government investment, they
are not, as far as we know, directly funding

00:20:04.289 --> 00:20:10.710
large-scale quantum computing hardware development
in the way that these other companies are.

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:15.820
But the government does provide a lot of R&amp;D
support from quantum computing and other quantum

00:20:15.820 --> 00:20:20.320
technologies, mostly through the National
Quantum Initiative, which was signed into

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:21.760
law in 2018.

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:25.660
SIERRA: The National Quantum Initiative is
an Act of Congress.

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:31.170
It gives the United States a plan for advancing
quantum computing in the name of safeguarding

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:33.980
national and economic security.

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:37.630
SIERRA: So what other countries are working
on this?

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:45.880
GHOSE: I think by now almost every major government
or conglomerate is looking into quantum kinds

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:47.820
of applications and technologies.

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:52.940
So the U.S. has certainly announced a couple
of rounds of multi-billion dollar funding

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:55.760
for this on the research sector side.

00:20:55.760 --> 00:20:56.780
India has announced funding.

00:20:56.780 --> 00:20:59.210
The European Union has its own funding.

00:20:59.210 --> 00:21:04.039
China has huge amounts of funding that they're
pouring into it, which dwarfs everybody else.

00:21:04.039 --> 00:21:08.570
SIERRA: Many see the US’s biggest quantum
competitor as China.

00:21:08.570 --> 00:21:12.950
And right now, China is building a $10 billion
quantum supercenter.

00:21:12.950 --> 00:21:19.779
DW News: China has announced their development
of the power quantum computer in the world,

00:21:19.779 --> 00:21:24.059
President Xi Jingping has said that research
and development in quantum science is an urgent

00:21:24.059 --> 00:21:28.659
matter of national concern and the country
has invested heavily in this technology.

00:21:28.659 --> 00:21:31.230
It has become a world leader in the field.

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:34.870
WEBER: There is definitely a competition.

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:42.299
A lot of the policy conversations these days
certainly have a geopolitical competition

00:21:42.299 --> 00:21:45.010
lens behind them.

00:21:45.010 --> 00:21:51.320
In the U.S., of course, there is a big focus
on the competition with China and we've seen

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:54.720
impressive results coming out of China in
the past couple of years.

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:58.031
You know, it's clear that they're putting
huge investments into the technology, they're

00:21:58.031 --> 00:21:59.909
making a lot of progress.

00:21:59.909 --> 00:22:05.210
It is something that I think we need to be
really careful that these geopolitical concerns

00:22:05.210 --> 00:22:07.820
don't stifle scientific collaboration.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:10.850
As we've been emphasizing throughout this
conversation, we are at a very early stage

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:11.970
in this technology.

00:22:11.970 --> 00:22:18.260
It's really still at the fundamental research
stage, and one thing that we know is that

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:23.750
collaboration is super important for advancing
fundamental research.

00:22:23.750 --> 00:22:31.300
So, you know, policies that can foster collaboration
amongst like-minded countries to more quickly

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:35.830
realize the impacts of quantum computing,
I think, are super important.

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:40.640
As somebody who has worked in science policy,
I mean it's at the heart of the broader U.S.

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:45.530
approach to research and innovation, and it's
what has established us as a leader.

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:48.279
I think we don't want to lose that.

00:22:48.279 --> 00:22:54.390
SIERRA: Every country wants to get ahead in
the quantum game, but too much emphasis on

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:57.740
competition and secrecy could stifle progress.

00:22:57.740 --> 00:23:03.059
Part of the balancing act is prioritizing
security while also working with the brightest

00:23:03.059 --> 00:23:09.260
minds abroad, and when possible, bringing
those minds to the U.S. through smart immigration

00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:10.260
policy.

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:11.620
GIUSTINA: It's already hard to recruit.

00:23:11.620 --> 00:23:18.299
We already are kind of low on experts, and
that is a big critical piece of being able

00:23:18.299 --> 00:23:23.419
to develop the technology, so any way in which
we restrict ourselves is a hindrance.

00:23:23.419 --> 00:23:29.400
We really need to be able to access as many
brains as possible and nurture and nourish

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:35.930
as many brains as possible to make them available
for this kind of technology development.

00:23:35.930 --> 00:23:42.309
SIERRA: As we think about who is at the table,
as this gets built, I sort of want to pivot

00:23:42.309 --> 00:23:45.110
to something else and ask you about it.

00:23:45.110 --> 00:23:52.700
Much of the STEM world is still dominated
by men, AI, blockchain, you know everything.

00:23:52.700 --> 00:23:57.960
So we even did a whole episode about STEM
and I, myself, spent a few years in tech.

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:05.480
We've seen in the past what happens when technology
is built by one relatively homogeneous group.

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:11.169
So my question is do you think, or do you
have concerns about that, that could be applied

00:24:11.169 --> 00:24:14.210
to the quantum computing space?

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:20.529
GHOSE: Yes, it's already happening that the
same homogenous group is developing the technology

00:24:20.529 --> 00:24:22.279
and yes, of course it's an issue.

00:24:22.279 --> 00:24:27.730
So I was looking at some of the sort of founding
boards and so on, the executive level membership

00:24:27.730 --> 00:24:29.370
and leadership of these companies.

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:35.460
And it is shockingly homogenous, in the sense
that almost every board I looked at was all

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:37.580
white men.

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:42.029
And that, on one hand, that's not surprising,
but on the other hand, it's also depressing

00:24:42.029 --> 00:24:48.169
that it's continuing the culture of what we
already see in Silicon Valley, for example.

00:24:48.169 --> 00:24:51.850
There is more conversation, I will say, around
these issues.

00:24:51.850 --> 00:24:59.520
People are trying to find ways to open up
the field more and bring in a more diverse

00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:03.950
group for the workforce, but it's slow.

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:09.260
And until we have change at the leadership
level, I don't think these approaches will

00:25:09.260 --> 00:25:10.590
be anything more than token.

00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:12.640
And I see a lot of that happen.

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:18.620
SIERRA: There’s still time to shape quantum
computing - who builds it, who has access

00:25:18.620 --> 00:25:22.399
to it, and how we protect ourselves and our
privacy.

00:25:22.399 --> 00:25:27.190
Time to ensure that their enormous power is
directed at the right problems, and includes

00:25:27.190 --> 00:25:28.190
everyone.

00:25:28.190 --> 00:25:32.880
SIERRA: It’s all pretty exciting, also confusing,
and a little scary, but exciting.

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:39.169
GHOSE: If we do build better technologies
and we are at some point able to build structures

00:25:39.169 --> 00:25:47.750
and processes to be using and making these
kinds of technologies accessible to everybody

00:25:47.750 --> 00:25:52.230
then I feel, of course, that could unlock
a future that would be amazing.

00:25:52.230 --> 00:25:55.470
And I'd love to be able to be part of that.

00:25:55.470 --> 00:25:58.480
SIERRA: You just make me excited about this.

00:25:58.480 --> 00:26:04.919
I really, you know, if podcasting doesn't
work out, I'll start my new life as a quantum

00:26:04.919 --> 00:26:05.919
physicist.

00:26:05.919 --> 00:26:07.230
GHOSE: Come on over anytime.

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:09.289
Or maybe I'll become a podcaster.

00:26:09.289 --> 00:26:10.289
SIERRA: Exactly.

00:26:10.289 --> 00:26:20.549
GHOSE: And where we go, we just do it together.

00:26:20.549 --> 00:26:23.789
SIERRA: Yeah!

